ABOUT: Welcome to the commentary track!

rtn:V-VERSION: Print the title, release, and serial number.
	In 1982, Infocom briefly branded its games as “INTERLOGIC”. They had mostly switched to “interactive fiction” by 1984. In fact, in this game’s archived source code, they were in the process of deleting the “INTERLOGIC” trademark.
	(For consistency, I have reverted that change in the source code display. See [comment|~src:WITNESS-4].)

src:WITNESS-4: Yes, I added that comment. As it says, I had to revert a few source code changes to get it to match the r23 game file. See [rtn:V-VERSION], for example.

rtn:V-HELP: Earlier releases of the game referred to the manual as “your Nat’l Detective Gazette”. (Which is how the manual was portrayed in the folio edition.) The conceit was dropped in the grey-box edition (release 21).
	A future release would have added a nudge about buying the InvisiClues. For consistency, I have reverted that change in the source code display. (See [comment|~src:WITNESS-4].)

src:VERBS-222: See [rtn:TANDY?]

rtn:TANDY?: The TRS-80 interpreter set a special bit which told the game to change certain messages. *The Witness* has more Tandy modifications than any other Infocom game. It adds the line “Licensed to Tandy Corporation”, and also (sort of) cleans up some period 1930s jargon. (Changing “private dick” to “private eye”, for example.)
	For a complete list of “Tandy bit” behavior changes in Infocom games, [see this page|https://ifarchive.org/if-archive/infocom/info/tandy_bits.html].
	To see the Tandy bit in action, type the magic command `$DB.$TA.$DB`.

GRAMMAR-LEGEND: This is the list of verb forms which the game recognizes. (Simple directions like `NORTH` are a special case; they’re not listed here.) These are compiled from the [SYNTAX|~src:SYNTAX-36] file.
	Objects in a verb line have various flags which give hints about their usage. An object attribute (like `LIGHTBIT`) means that if the player doesn’t name an object, the parser will try to guess one with that attribute.
	The other flag letters have specific meanings: the object must be **h**eld; the object will be **t**aken if not held; the object should be **i**n the room or **o**n the ground; the object should be **p**ossessed or **c**arried in a container. (Yes, they’re confusing. Infocom’s own docs say “No one really understands them except Stu” (Galley).)

attr:RMUNGBIT: Not applied to real objects. `RMUNGBIT` is a hack used in grammar lines; it means the object is optional (but the preposition before it is required). This allows the parser to accept commands like [LOOK AROUND|~src:SYNTAX-291].

glob:P-SYNTAX: The grammar line found when the most recent command was parsed. (Except for simple direction commands, which are a special case.) See the Grammar tab.


OBJTREE-LEGEND: In this list, `Rooms` are rooms and `Items` are regular items. (Sometimes portable, sometimes not.)
	`Scen` indicates scenery objects. These exist in multiple places; for example, the [~obj:HOUSE] is visible from most of the outdoors areas.
	`Glob` indicates a global object, which is *always* visible, or at least mentionable. For example, [~obj:GLOBAL-MURDER] is global, so you can always `ASK PERSON ABOUT MURDER`.

obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS: This is a container for scenery objects like [~obj:HOUSE], which are visible from many locations in the game.


glob:WINNER: `WINNER` is the actor in the current command.
	`WINNER` is usually [~obj:PLAYER], but for a command like `PHONG, GO EAST` it briefly shifts to the named NPC.

glob:HERE: The location of the [*glob:WINNER].

glob:SCORE: The hours value of the current time. (See also [glob:PRESENT-TIME].)
	The Z-machine was originally designed to show `SCORE` and `MOVES` in the status line. In *The Witness*, these variables were repurposed to show a time in hours and minutes. A bit in the game file header tells the interpreter which way to format them.

glob:MOVES: The minutes value of the current time. (See also [glob:PRESENT-TIME].)
	The Z-machine was originally designed to show `SCORE` and `MOVES` in the status line. In *The Witness*, these variables were repurposed to show a time in hours and minutes. A bit in the game file header tells the interpreter which way to format them.

glob:PRESENT-TIME: The current time, stored as minutes after noon (`H*60+M`). The game starts at 8:00 pm.
	See also [glob:SCORE] and [glob:MOVES], which track the current time in a different way.

rtn:GO: Set up the world at the start of the game. Start the timers. Print the banner. Set all those useful globals like [~glob:HERE] and [~glob:WINNER].

rtn:WELCOME: Why do you have exactly twelve hours to solve the murder? At least one reason is so that the game does not have to parse “am” or “pm” times. `WAIT UNTIL 9:30` unambiguously refers to the evening, because you can’t stay until 9:30 in the morning.

glob:LIT: Whether the current location has light. *The Witness* has no dark areas so this is always true.

glob:LOW-DIRECTION: Properties with this value and above are directions.

glob:LOAD-MAX: The weight limit you can carry. This is always 100.

glob:LOAD-ALLOWED: Your current weight limit. This is a carry-over from the Zork games, where you can carry less when injured. The [~rtn:ITAKE] routine checks this condition, but nothing in the game can injure you.

glob:P-WON: Set if the parser successfully parsed a command. (As opposed to a blank line or an error.)
	No relation to whether the *player* has won.

glob:P-ADVERB: Unlike other Infocom games (and nearly all modern IF), *The Witness* pays attention to some adverbs. You can `EXAMINE` an object, or you can `EXAMINE` it `CAREFULLY` or `CLOSELY`. The parser will also [take note|~src:PARSER-317] of the words `QUIETLY`, `SLOWLY`, `QUICKLY`, and `BRIEFLY`.
	`EXAMINE CAREFULLY` takes several minutes instead of one, but in a few cases it reveals more information. See [~rtn:CLOCK-F], [~rtn:MONICA-TABLE-F].
	You can also `COMPARE` things `CAREFULLY` to verify a match. [~rtn:STILES-SHOES-F], [~rtn:MUDDY-SHOES-F], [~rtn:INSIDE-GUN-F], [~rtn:SPOOL-OF-WIRE-F].

src:PARSER-317: Unlike other Infocom games (and nearly all modern IF), *The Witness* pays attention to some adverbs. See [glob:P-ADVERB].

glob:C-INTS: Indicates how much of the timer table ([*glob:C-TABLE]) is used. This counts down from `C-TABLELEN`, which is 300.

glob:C-DEMONS: Indicates how much of the timer table is used for daemons.
	The difference between daemons and timers is that timers run every turn; daemons run every *input*, even if no turn passes. But *The Witness* does not use daemons.

glob:C-TABLE: The timer table. See the “Timers” tab for a current listing.

TIMERS-LEGEND: A list of the timers running (or paused) in the timer table ([~glob:C-TABLE]). Positive numbers count down (while enabled) and call the named function after that many turns. Negative numbers are called every turn while the timer is enabled.
	This list only grows as you play; timers can be added, started, and stopped, but not removed.
	Note that enabled negative timers, like [~rtn:I-ATTENTION], count down towards “negative infinity”. They’ll wrap and stop working after 65535 turns, but back then nobody worried about this!


obj:OBJECT-PAIR: Multiple actions like `GET ALL` are usually handled one object at a time. However, *The Witness* allows you to `ARREST` two people as one action. (Yeah, that counts as a spoiler, sorry.)
	The [~rtn:MAIN-LOOP-1] has a special case for this [here|~src:MAIN-89]. The `ARREST` and `COMPARE` actions are performed with the special object `OBJECT-PAIR`, whose action function [rtn:OBJECT-PAIR-F] then handles the dual verb.

rtn:OBJECT-PAIR-F: The code which handles an action on two objects at once. (See [obj:OBJECT-PAIR].) In *The Witness*, this is only the `ARREST` action (see [rtn:ARREST]) and the `COMPARE` action.

rtn:ARREST:
	The code which handles arresting someone (or sometwo).
	For a complete breakdown of the endgame logic, see [SOLUTION].

ABOUT-SOLUTION: Okay, secretly there is. But I’ve put it behind a spoiler block.
	Are you sure you want to see a complete breakdown of the endgame logic?
	[Yes, I’m sure|SHOW-SOLUTION-TAB].

SOLUTION: Are you sure you want to see a complete breakdown of the endgame logic?
	[Yes, I’m sure|SHOW-SOLUTION-TAB].

SHOW-SOLUTION-TAB: A “Solution” tab has been added to the right pane.


glob:ANALYSIS-OBJ: Whether Duffy’s current task is `FINGERPRINT` or `ANALYZE`.


glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS: The day’s schedule for each character.
	Monica and Phong each have a list of movement times and goals. A line `70 10 KITCHEN` means that they will wait 70±10 minutes (60 to 80) and then begin moving towards the `KITCHEN`.
	(Stiles has some movement entries but they are all commented out.)

glob:GOAL-TABLES: The current movement goal of each character. The scheme is explained in the “Schedule” tab.

rtn:IN-MOTION?: Whether a character is moving towards a destination, as determined by [glob:GOAL-TABLES].

rtn:IMOVEMENT: Prepare a character to move towards their next destination. The character’s timer routine calls this when it is time for them to “wake up” and move.
	This calls [rtn:ESTABLISH-GOAL] to set up their movement. Then they advance one line on their [~glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS] table and set a long timer (30 or 60 minutes or whatever) – the delay until their *next* wake-up call.

# More precisely:
# - if not first move (initial 0 entry), establish-goal
# - if not last entry (final 0 time), pick the interval, queue timer, advance ptr

rtn:I-FOLLOW: Move each character towards their current goal, if they ought to be in motion.

rtn:FOLLOW-GOAL: Move a character one step towards their current goal. All the work driven by the [~glob:GOAL-TABLES] happens in this routine.

rtn:ESTABLISH-GOAL: Set up a character’s [~glob:GOAL-TABLES] entry to move them towards a destination. Normally this is driven by the [~glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS] table. If the `PRIORITY` argument is set, this is a plot-critical action which overrides the normal schedule.
	See the “Schedule” tab for a complete explanation.

rtn:INT-WAIT: Run the game time forward by `N` minutes. Returns true if the full time elapsed, or false if the player chose to stop waiting (because of some observed action).
	This is used by a couple of actions that take more than one minute: `EXAMINE CAREFULLY`, `EXAMINE ROOM`. If you don’t wait the full interval, the `EXAMINE` action fails.

prop:LINE: The transit line which a given room is on or adjacent to. See [~prop:STATION].

prop:STATION: The transit-line room which is, or is adjacent to, a given room. See [~prop:LINE].

glob:OUTSIDE-LINE: These tables define the four “transit lines” which let NPCs travel around the map. Every room is either on or adjacent to a transit line.

rtn:I-ATTENTION: This timer routine decreases each character’s attention level after you call their name or do something else which calls [~rtn:GRAB-ATTENTION]. These values are stored in [glob:ATTENTION-TABLE]. After two to nine minutes, they will become impatient and leave.

glob:ATTENTION-TABLE: The current attention level of each character. This is set positive if you call their name or do something else which calls [~rtn:GRAB-ATTENTION]. It then decreases every turn ([rtn:I-ATTENTION]) to zero, at which point they wander off.
	(In fact the values in this table decrease *every* turn, becoming negative. Negative values are ignored. For simplicity, I display negative values as “0” in the Schedule tab.)

glob:COR-1: The six `COR-N` tables define six corridors of rooms that have sight-lines to each other. (Some of the “corridors” are outdoors.) Each room in a corridor has that bit set in its [prop:CORRIDOR] property.
	This permits the game to display messages like “Mr. Phong, off to the north, ducks into a room to the north.”

prop:CORRIDOR: This property defines one or more “corridors” (sight-lines) that the player can see down from a given room. See [glob:COR-1].

glob:WHERE-TABLES: This tracks when each character last saw each other character. This lets you ask questions like `LINDER, WHERE IS PHONG?` (You won’t get the location, but you will get a time.)


glob:DIFFICULTY: Apparently early versions of the game had `EASIER` and `HARDER` commands, which allowed the player to make some clues more or less obvious. The idea was dropped during development. (See [this letter|https://github.com/historicalsource/witness/blob/master/letter.mss] archived with the source code.)
	All that remains of this feature is some commented-out code for the `V-EASIER` and `V-HARDER` commands, the placement of the [broken glass|~src:PLACES-768], and a [bullet hole|src:PLACES-2310].

src:PLACES-2310: Early versions of the game had an `EASIER` mode, in which you could find the bullet-hole and figure out the angle of the shot. The idea was dropped during development. See [glob:DIFFICULTY].


obj:PHONG: Phong’s full name is Hui Phong, or possibly Phong Hui. This is never mentioned in the game at all. (It appears in the original [design document|https://github.com/historicalsource/witness/blob/master/witnes.rno].)

obj:STILES: Stiles’s first name is Ralph. This is not mentioned in the game except in the post-arrest report, e.g. [here|~src:EVENTS-1707].

rtn:FIX-PHONG-MOVEMENT: Phong’s movement schedule does not begin at the start of the game. It only kicks off after he leads you to the dining room (and then leaves). Because the [glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS] table stores the time of the character’s *next* movement, Phong’s entries have to be adjusted to allow for his late start.

glob:TOO-LATE: If you don’t enter the house before Linder finishes his drink, his plan is ruined. He will refuse to let you in after that point.
	On the up side, Linder doesn’t get killed. On the down side, he rubs out Stiles a few days later. See [rtn:TOO-LATE-F].

rtn:TOO-LATE-F: The game ends with this addendum if you never enter the house.

glob:PLAYER-NEAR-SHOT: The last time you examined the keyhole. If Linder triggers the gun and you just did this, you get it right in the eye. See [~rtn:FIRE-SHOT].

obj:CAT: Poor Asta’s only function is to disappear when the gun goes off. She never returns.


rtn:I-WEATHER: Weather timer. The sequence of events is coded into the routine, rather than being a table.

rtn:I-GOTO-LIVING-ROOM: Transports you to the living room a moment after you enter the house.

rtn:I-LINDER-WELCOME: Despite the name, this is not a timer routine. (It might have been one in an earlier revision.) It simply handles the introductions when [rtn:I-GOTO-LIVING-ROOM] fires.

rtn:I-LINDER-FOLLOWS-YOU: Timer routine for (surprise) Linder following you around. Starts when he welcomes you to the house ([rtn:I-LINDER-WELCOME]); ends when [rtn:I-LINDER-TO-OFFICE] fires.

rtn:I-LINDER-TO-OFFICE: Transports you to the office when Linder is ready to talk. Unless you’re wandering outside at the time, in which case he gives up on you ([glob:TOO-LATE]).

rtn:I-LINDER-IN-OFFICE: Linder pesters you to sit down.

rtn:I-LINDER-EXPLAIN: Linder’s spiel.
	Once this fires, you have nothing to do but interrogate Linder until Stiles arrives on the office porch (see [~rtn:I-STILES]).

rtn:I-STILES-ARRIVE: The timer for Stiles arriving on the scene. This places him in the [~obj:OFFICE-PATH] and then sets his destination to the [~obj:OFFICE-PORCH]. When Stiles reaches there, his general timer routine ([rtn:I-STILES]) will kick off the main event.

rtn:I-LINDER-RETIRES: Linder goes to bed. This can only happen in the [glob:TOO-LATE] case, of course.

rtn:I-PHONG-RETIRES: Phong goes to bed. This can only happen in the [glob:TOO-LATE] case; after the shooting he’s too wound up to sleep.

rtn:I-STILES: The timer routine which handles Stiles’s overall actions. Other routines establish destinations for him ([rtn:I-STILES-ARRIVE] and so on). This routine reacts when he arrives. For example, when Stiles reaches the [~obj:OFFICE-PORCH], Linder enacts his plan. When he reaches the [~obj:LIVING-ROOM] (dragged by Duffy), he gets handcuffed to the davenport.

glob:DUFFY-WITH-STILES: Sgt. Duffy has no representation in the game. (He has a topic object, [~obj:GLOBAL-DUFFY], but that never appears on stage.)
	When Duffy hauls Stiles from the entry to the living room, it’s really just Stiles moving of his own accord. The `DUFFY-WITH-STILES` flag describes him as being led by Duffy.


src:EVENTS-1143: `"Suddenly Phong appears and "` is a separate string, although the next string is printed directly after – the player can’t see the break.
	This may have been an attempt at optimization. A few lines down, the next `TELL` is split at the same point. This pattern appears very frequently across the source code.
	The ZIL compiler did not in fact optimize this case. The string appears twice in the compiled routine, not once.


glob:MONICA-HAS-MOTIVE: When set, indicates exactly what revealed Monica’s resentment of her father. But the reason is never used; the [rtn:ARREST] logic only cares whether this has been set or not.

glob:PHONG-HAS-MOTIVE: When set, indicates exactly what revealed Phong’s resentment of his employer. But this value is never used at all.

src:EVENTS-1774: There is a gap in the `ARREST` logic here. If you have discovered the gun mechanism and the [~obj:GUN-RECEIPT], but not uncovered Monica’s motive or her clock key, then `ARREST MONICA AND PHONG` gives the response:
	“While Monica could have conspired with him and aided him, she too was acquitted of conspiracy, because”
	It would be appropriate to finish “...she had no apparent motive,” but the game fails to do that.


rtn:V-PHONE: The telephone cheats a bit. The city codes `CHANDLER` and `ADELINE` (or `CH` and `AD`) are in the [BUZZ|~src:SYNTAX-6] list, like `A`, `AN`, and `THE`. `BUZZ` words are completely ignored by the parser. If you type `CALL CHANDLER 1729`, only the numeric part matters.


src:MAIN-45: At some point the ZIL compiler gained the ability to inline routines. The `MAIN-LOOP` routine does not appear in the game file; I have greyed it out to indicate this. The code inside the routine *does* exist, however. It is placed inside [rtn:GO] above.

src:THINGS-426: At some point the ZIL compiler gained the ability to inline routines. The `SEEKING-DRINK? ` routine does not appear in the game file; I have greyed it out to indicate this. The code inside the routine *does* exist, however. It is placed inside [~rtn:LIQUOR-F] below.

src:PLACES-781: You originally had to examine the window `CAREFULLY` to discover the green wire, but this requirement has been commented out.

obj:DRIVEWAY-GATE: No `DRIVEWAY-GATE` object is defined in the source! But the [~obj:PLAYER] is defined as being inside it. (Although you are moved to [~obj:DRIVEWAY-ENTRANCE] before the first move.)
	Because of that declaration, the compiler generated an anonymous object with no properties. It cannot be referred to and has no effect in the game.

src:EVENTS-606: The table refers to an `I-LINDER` routine, but no such routine exists! The same goes for `I-CAT`.
	The compiler silently filled in zeroes for these table entries. Linder (and the cat) are controlled by hand-coded timer code, not by the [glob:GOAL-TABLES].

src:PLACES-2630: An office bathroom, commented out.
	Note that the `ACTION` property refers to `LIBRARY-BALCONY-F`, which is a *Deadline* room name. This code must have been copied over and barely edited before it was discarded.
	
src:PLACES-2889: This commented-out scenery object is pasted directly from *Deadline*.

src:WITNESS-49: The game defines diagonal directions, but they have no [SYNONYM|src:SYNTAX-22] lines and thus are not recognized by the parser.

src:SYNTAX-22: The `SYNONYM` lines for the diagonal directions are commented out. So the parser does not recognize them, even though they’re defined in the [DIRECTIONS|src:WITNESS-49] line.

src:PLACES-947: The `)>` characters on this line are unbalanced; they do not match any earlier syntax. 
	Any modern language would scream bloody murder about this kind of mistake, but apparently Infocom’s ZIL compiler silently ignored it. So did the MDL compiler that it was based on. See [discussion|https://intfiction.org/t/question-about-infocom-zil-syntax/80690/9].
	(The `[` character is *not* a mistake; it’s paired with a `]` character [here|~src:PLACES-1083]. See the [note about brackets|src:PLACES-392].)

src:PLACES-392: Big chunks of this file are enclosed with square brackets. (Here to [here|~src:PLACES-439], for example.) Why?
	The brackets don’t seem to affect compilation at all. Our best guess is that they were a text editor convenience, allowing the developer to jump across groups of related rooms to the matching bracket.
	It’s also possible that the developer wanted to comment out groups of rooms. In ZIL, a semicolon in front of a pair of delimiters would have commented out everything between them.

src:THINGS-262: The adjective list includes `HAND`, but the dictionary word `HAND` is labelled only as a verb, not an adjective. This inconsistency prevents the adjective from working.

